{"cuisine/naga-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/naga-cuisine","n":"Naga cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Naga cuisine consists of foods from or commonly eaten by the Naga people in Northeast India and Kachin, Sagaing Region of Myanmar,  or by the Naga diaspora. Each ethnic group of the Nagas prepares its own unique style of cuisines. It most notably features rice, meats and leaf vegetables. Meat prepared by the Nagas are often smoked, dried or fermented. While Naga cuisine is part of South Asian cuisine due to national boundaries, it is closer to Southeast Asian cuisine, especially Burmese cuisine and Northern Thailand cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"naga-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naga_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Roots and Leisure (Mizo dishes)","ref":"site/rootsandleisure-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"rootsandleisure.com","note":"Northeast India food and culture site, with Naga recipes and profiles of Naga cooks.","url":"https://rootsandleisure.com/tag/naga-cuisine/","suggested":true},{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","ref":"site/archanaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Nagaland section of Archana Doshi's Indian recipe site.","url":"https://www.archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/nagaland","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Naga Cuisine: Ethnic Flavours","ref":"cookbook/naga-cuisine-ethnic-flavours","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Rovi Chasie","note":"Traditional Naga recipes by a Nagaland chef, reissued in a new edition in 2023.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Akhuni","dish/akhuni"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2b/Naga_Thali_-_Student_Dhaba_-_Meghalaya_DSC_011.jpg/500px-Naga_Thali_-_Student_Dhaba_-_Meghalaya_DSC_011.jpg","w":500,"h":744,"by":"Gitika Gayan","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naga_Thali_-_Student_Dhaba_-_Meghalaya_DSC_011.jpg"}},"cuisine/calabrian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/calabrian-cuisine","n":"Calabrian cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Calabria is a region in Southern Italy. It is a peninsula bordered by the Basilicata region to the north, the Ionian Sea to the east, the Strait of Messina to the southwest, which separates it from Sicily, and the Tyrrhenian Sea to the west. It has a population of 1,827,571 across a total area of 15,221.90 km2 (5,877.21 sq mi). Catanzaro is the region's capital."],["Calabria is the birthplace of the name of Italy, given to it by the Ancient Greeks who settled in this land starting from the 8th century BCE. They established the first cities, mainly on the coast, as Greek colonies. During this period Calabria was the heart of Magna Graecia, home of key figures in history such as Pythagoras, Herodotus and Milo."],["In Roman times, it was part of the Regio III Lucania et Bruttii, a region of Augustan Italy. After the Gothic War, it became and remained for five centuries a Byzantine dominion, fully recovering its Greek character. Cenobitism flourished, with the rise throughout the peninsula of numerous churches, hermitages and monasteries in which Basilian monks were dedicated to transcription. The Byzantines introduced the art of silk in Calabria and made it the main silk production area in Europe. In the 11th century, the Norman conquest started a slow process of Latinization."],["Calabria has three historical ethnolinguistic minorities: the Grecanici, speaking Calabrian Greek; the Arbëreshë people, speaking Albanian language; and the Occitans of Guardia Piemontese. This linguistic diversity makes the region an object of study for linguists from all over the world."],["Calabria is famous for its crystal clear sea waters and is dotted with ancient villages, castles and archaeological parks. Three national parks are found in the region: the Pollino National Park (which is the largest in Italy), the Sila National Park and the Aspromonte National Park."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"calabrian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calabrian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cooking with Rosetta","ref":"site/cookingwithrosetta-bureaujules-squarespace-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookingwithrosetta-bureaujules.squarespace.com","note":"Calabrian-born cooking teacher Rosetta Costantino's blog on Calabrian food.","url":"https://cookingwithrosetta-bureaujules.squarespace.com/blog-1","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"My Calabria: Rustic Family Cooking from Italy's Undiscovered South","ref":"cookbook/my-calabria-rustic-family-cooking-from-italy-s-undiscovered-south","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Rosetta Costantino, 2010","note":"Calabrian family recipes and regional background, published by W. W. Norton.","url":"https://wwnorton.co.uk/books/9780393065169-my-calabria","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/4/4b/Calabria.jpg","w":500,"h":495,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calabria.jpg"}},"dish/ajika":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ajika","n":"Ajika","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Georgian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ajika is a flavored sauce or dip made mainly in Abkhazia and Mingrelia regions (Georgia). It is often used to flavor food. Ajika is primarily capsicum-based and usually includes other spices such as coriander or utskho suneli. Common varieties of ajika resemble Italian red pesto in appearance and consistency, although a dry version also exists. Though it is usually red, green ajika is also made with unripe peppers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ajika"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajika"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Herbs","ingredient/herbs"],["Khmeli suneli","ingredient/khmeli-suneli"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Georgian Recipes","ref":"site/georgianrecipes-net","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"georgianrecipes.net","note":"Recipe site dedicated to traditional Georgian dishes.","url":"https://georgianrecipes.net/about-georgian-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Georgian cuisine","links":[["Achma","dish/achma"],["Akhasheni","dish/akhasheni"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"],["Chakhokhbili","dish/chakhokhbili"],["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"],["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"],["Churchkhela","dish/churchkhela"],["Dambalkhacho","dish/dambalkhacho"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"],["Khachapuri","dish/khachapuri"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khvanchkara","dish/khvanchkara"],["Kindzmarauli","dish/kindzmarauli"],["Kubdari","dish/kubdari"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"],["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"],["Mujuretuli","dish/mujuretuli"],["Nadugi","dish/nadugi"],["Nigvziani badrijani","dish/nigvziani-badrijani"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"],["Tsolikouri","dish/tsolikouri"],["Tvishi (wine)","dish/tvishi-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/58/Adjika_e-citizen.jpg/500px-Adjika_e-citizen.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Moonsun1981","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adjika_e-citizen.jpg"}},"ingredient/mussel-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mussel-as-food","n":"Mussel as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Mussel () is the common name used for members of several families of bivalve molluscs, from saltwater and freshwater habitats. These groups have in common a shell whose outline is elongated and asymmetrical compared with other edible clams, which are often more or less rounded or oval."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mussel-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mussel_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cockle as food","ingredient/cockle-as-food"],["Oyster as food","ingredient/oyster-as-food"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]]}]},"dish/bacalhau-a-minhota":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bacalhau-a-minhota","n":"Bacalhau à minhota","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Bacalhau à minhota is a traditional dish from the Alto Minho region in northern Portugal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bacalhau-a-minhota"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacalhau_%C3%A0_minhota